00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.263
So a brief overview.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.490
What is Jenkins, this Jenkins
guy we've been talking about?

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:03.480
It's a build server.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.690
You can trigger or
schedule jobs or processes

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.820
and run your automation steps.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.830
At some point, somebody
has scripted something,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.840
and they want to run it
in a shared environment

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.130
or run it on every commit.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.630
Jenkins gives you a really
easy way to do that.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.050
We also have a
pluggable architecture,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.739
which is a lot of the
reason why Jenkins

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:22.530
is used by a lot of
the folks in this room.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.740
It can capture a lot of
the use cases that we have

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.500
and various tools that we need
to integrate into our CI and CD

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.730
processes.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.580
How many people use
Jenkins plugins?

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.670
How many people use more
than five Jenkins plugins?

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.740
More than 10?

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.535
20?

00:02:39.535 --> 00:02:40.034
Ooh.

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:40.533
All right.

00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:43.620
We should talk after.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.110
The Jenkins plugin ecosystem is
what enables us to use Jenkins,

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:49.000
but also has its downsides.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.870
You have to keep
track of the plugins.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.279
They are mostly open source.

00:02:52.279 --> 00:02:54.070
You have to know the
quality of the plugin.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.690
It has to have
good documentation.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.770
It has to have a good
upgrade strategy.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:00.190
So with every blessing,
there is a little bit

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:03.460
of a curse in these plugins.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.100
One of the nice things about
Jenkins is it runs on the JVM

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.620
and so does its agent,
so we can register nodes

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.520
on various distros.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.100
So you can have Linux agents--
which is what mostly I've used

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:12.850
in the past--

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.230
Windows agents, or Mac agents.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:18.021
How many people are using
Windows or Mac with Jenkins?

00:03:18.021 --> 00:03:18.935
Yeah.

00:03:18.935 --> 00:03:19.850
Cool.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.516
So that's what we're
talking about here.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.760
So when someone asked me, what
do I do for my CI/CD process?

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.387
I just sprinkle a
little Jenkins on it.

00:03:30.387 --> 00:03:32.470
That's not always the case,
but a lot of the times

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:33.261
we can start there.

00:03:33.261 --> 00:03:36.850
So what does a Jenkins
journey look like?

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.730
Well, day one, genesis.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.790
People have been running
scripts on their computers.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.070
There's some scripts
that are clobbering

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.965
each other's behaviors,
and you decide

00:03:46.965 --> 00:03:49.090
that you need a shared
place to run these based off

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:52.160
of get commits, which would be
a really nice way to do things.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.880
So you find a machine somewhere.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.210
It's probably in the corner.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.830
It's like a Raspberry Pi.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.750
And you install Java on it.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.010
It takes up all the memory.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:01.669
You install Jenkins.

00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:03.460
You load up all your
dependencies on there,

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:04.834
whatever you need
for your build.

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:07.030
You might need a special
version of Node or Go

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.054
or some test library
or something like that.

00:04:09.054 --> 00:04:10.720
And then, you start
running your scripts

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.020
in this shared build server.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.090
So you're stoked.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:17.170
It's better than everybody
else running in their laptops.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:20.500
And what you end up with is this
one Jenkins master node that

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.250
has the Jenkins JVM process
in there, a build executer--

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.070
that's a process that's
actually running your builds--

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.810
and all your build
dependencies in there.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:33.020
Additionally, you have the
Jenkins home file system.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.980
How many people are
familiar with Jenkins home?

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:36.980
It's really comfortable.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.540
It's our home.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.390
So in your home, you
have your configuration,

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.610
and that's the configuration
of all your plugins,

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.840
of your Jenkins server itself.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.590
You also have built artifacts--
so things that you're storing

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.080
after you're build completes.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.304
That might be some sort of
binary or things like that.

00:04:53.304 --> 00:04:55.220
The other thing that
runs in that Jenkins home

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.867
is your build workspaces.

00:04:56.867 --> 00:04:59.450
So your build workspaces is that
scratch space that your build

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:02.840
gets to, clone your repo, and
do temporary things in that

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.320
build process.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:06.435
So you're stoked.

00:05:06.435 --> 00:05:08.060
People are sharing
in this environment.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:11.660
They're triggering builds off
of commits or scheduling builds.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.430
But then, you see this, right?

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.070
How many people have
seen this Jenkins?

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:17.030
Yeah.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:17.600
It's painful.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.710
So something has gone wrong
in your Jenkins installation.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:23.990
It may not be scaling
appropriately.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.750
Maybe you ran out of resources
on that Raspberry Pi.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:28.770
So what do you do?

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:30.930
You scale two more nodes.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.660
So you take your build
agents, and you run them

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:34.420
somewhere else.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.337
So your builds run separately.

00:05:36.337 --> 00:05:36.920
This is great.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.600
You've moved your build executer
process out of your Jenkins

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.230
master.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:41.730
You've moved your
build dependencies

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.260
into those Jenkins nodes
and your build workspaces.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.900
So now you're Jenkins master
has some room to breathe.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.950
So this is a great next
step, having a static fleet

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:54.890
distributed from
your Jenkins master.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.650
But again, the
build is breaking.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:01.100
And this time, it is
because we need more disk.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.890
Those build artifacts that
were on our Jenkins master

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:07.500
have filled up our
Jenkins home file system.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.100
So now, we decide to
start using object storage

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.887
to move our artifacts off
of that Jenkins master.

00:06:12.887 --> 00:06:14.720
I think you should
probably use Google Cloud

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.540
Storage for reasons I
won't tell you about,

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.450
but you think you want
all of this storage

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:23.010
to move to any other place
other than your Jenkins masters,

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.260
and there's various plugins
that can make this happen.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.134
How many people are
using an artifact plugin?

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:31.650
OK.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:32.850
So now, you're happy.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.170
You're scaling well.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.060
Jenkins master is
healthy and happy.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.910
But your developers
are coming to you

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.100
and saying that
they need competing

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.100
versions of dependencies
on these build agents.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.350
So now, you've got the
problem of figuring out,

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:47.499
how do I label these nodes?

00:06:47.499 --> 00:06:49.290
How do I get people to
run the write builds

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:51.000
on the right systems?

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.640
But instead, you decide to
move to containerization.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:56.677
So you can run each of
your builds in containers,

00:06:56.677 --> 00:06:59.010
and you get the benefit of
being able to give developers

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.380
exactly the dependencies
that they want,

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.450
and you won't clobber
each other's dependencies.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:04.890
So now, developers are happy.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.850
They have more control.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.520
Next, they come to you
with the next problem.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:11.900
Yes, we have more control.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:12.710
This is great.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.480
But we're poking
around the Jenkins UI

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.380
too much in order to
configure our jobs.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.264
So we want a way to do
this in source code,

00:07:20.264 --> 00:07:21.680
and the way to do
this and Jenkins

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:24.140
is using the Jenkins
pipeline plugin

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:27.150
and creating your Jenkins
file next to your source code.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.000
So now, developers
can commit code

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.760
that changes the test
process or the build process

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.790
and the source code at the same
time, and they're really happy.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.351
How many people are
using Jenkins pipeline?

00:07:37.351 --> 00:07:37.850
Yes.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:42.240
We should get that to
everyone relatively soon.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:43.940
So that's great.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.400
And you put
pipelines everywhere.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.940
You can have your happy little
pipeline world in Jenkins land,

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:52.550
and everybody is happier that
they have control over their

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:54.990
build process.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:57.550
Next, your CFO comes
up to you and says,

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.490
hey, you know, it's
looking like we're

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.940
spending quite a bit of
money on this CI process.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.520
Do you think you could
tune that down in any way?

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.340
So you poke around and you
realize, oh, I know what to do.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:12.520
Between 8:00 and 6:00, I
have a lot of builds running.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.529
But as soon as developers
leave and go home,

00:08:14.529 --> 00:08:16.570
there's no builds running
on all my build agents.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:21.040
So you look into some way
to do autoscaling agents so

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.980
that, when you kick off a build
and there's no agent available,

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.650
it's provisioned for you, has
all your dependencies in there.

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:29.787
And then, when no builds are
running, it terminates itself.

00:08:29.787 --> 00:08:31.370
And there are various
ways to do this.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:34.539
There is a GCE plugin that
allows you to do this with VMs.

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:37.400
You can also use Kubernetes
plugin to do this on GKE,

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:40.110
and we'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:40.950
Great.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.280
So you're moving along at
a pretty good clip here.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:47.100
Now, day 412 comes
along, and each team

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.440
is competing for which
plugins are the right plugins

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.260
to have in their
Jenkins installation.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.350
Not every plugin is compatible
with every other plugin.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.450
There is a dependency
chain there.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:01.050
So they say, we want to have
our own Jenkins installations

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.850
for our team or for
our sets of teams.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:06.810
So you figure out a way to
make reproducible Jenkins

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.630
installations and give them
exactly the plugins that they

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.440
want.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.100
So you've made
everybody really happy.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.467
You have a scalable system.

00:09:14.467 --> 00:09:16.050
Now, you're managing
multiple Jenkins,

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:19.290
but since they autoscale and
they have enough resources

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:22.320
and don't overprovision
resources for them,

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.160
you're feeling good
as an administrator.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:26.660
OK.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:32.660
So that was what you need to
do to be happy with Jenkins

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:37.485
at scale, but how do
you get there today?

00:09:37.485 --> 00:09:41.530
First, you have to figure
out how to deploy Jenkins.

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:43.140
And in the previous
slide, I showed

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.900
using Helm to deploy Jenkins.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.400
One of the nice things--

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:49.159
how many people
know what Helm is?

00:09:49.159 --> 00:09:49.700
Get them out.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:51.241
So I'll talk a little
bit about Helm.

00:09:51.241 --> 00:09:54.380
Helm is a package
manager for Kubernetes.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:57.020
It allows you to package up
applications and then install

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:57.770
them.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.950
The packages in this
context are called charts,

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:04.520
and charts are ways to define
what comes with an application.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.130
There is a Jenkins home
chart in the community,

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:10.430
and it provides a lot
of the same defaults

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:12.440
that you would expect for
a Jenkins installation

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:15.720
to be scalable and secure.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.690
So we leverage this
in order to create

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:20.917
our installations of Jenkins.

00:10:20.917 --> 00:10:22.750
One of the nice things
about this Helm chart

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:24.850
is it allows you to
define declaratively

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.680
exactly the plugins
you want to have

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:30.514
in your installation,
which is super useful.

00:10:30.514 --> 00:10:31.680
So what does this look like?

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.210
On the left here, we have
the installation command

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:34.900
for Jenkins--

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.680
helm install stable/jenkins
and then a values file.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.130
Stable is the name of
the chart repository

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:42.840
that this chart is coming from.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.590
Jenkins is obviously
the name of the chart

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:45.520
that we're installing.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.061
And then, I pass it
this values file,

00:10:47.061 --> 00:10:48.890
and this is my configuration.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:51.147
On the right here, you
can see the configuration

00:10:51.147 --> 00:10:51.980
that I'm passing it.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.010
I have a list plugins.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:56.910
I have some CPU and
memory configuration.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:58.367
And I have some job ops.

00:10:58.367 --> 00:11:00.450
Who knows what people are
going to throw in there,

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:03.120
but that always comes up.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.620
The next thing I do is I disable
the default agent configuration

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:10.590
because I want my developers
in their Jenkins files

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:11.530
to define this.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:13.470
And I'll show how this
is possible later.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:16.330
The next few are
the persistence,

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:17.730
the size of the Jenkins home.

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:19.377
Again, I don't need
a huge disk, but I

00:11:19.377 --> 00:11:21.210
like to have some space
in there for Jenkins

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:24.580
to have its configurations
and so forth.

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:27.570
And the other two options,
the NetworkPolicy and the rbac

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:31.062
are some security defaults that
you can get out of the box.

00:11:31.062 --> 00:11:31.990
Cool.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.940
So what do I get by
installing this Helm

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:37.170
chart into a Kubernetes
engine cluster?

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:40.515
First, you can use Kubernetes
engine's autoscaling node

00:11:40.515 --> 00:11:44.190
pools, which allows you
to provision new pods,

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.080
and Kubernetes engine
will add new nodes

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.880
to your cluster as needed.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:51.750
Next, we get into that
world automatically where

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.924
we have all of our build
dependencies defined

00:11:53.924 --> 00:11:56.340
by containers-- that is, just
the default mode that you're

00:11:56.340 --> 00:12:00.072
going to use when you're
running on Kubernetes engine.

00:12:00.072 --> 00:12:02.030
The next thing you get
is ephemeral workspaces,

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:04.430
and this is something that
was really important when

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:07.070
I was running CI systems
a few years ago is

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:09.232
to have a clean workspace
for every build.

00:12:09.232 --> 00:12:10.940
So this is going to
be guaranteed for you

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:13.955
because your docker container is
going to come up, do its work,

00:12:13.955 --> 00:12:16.080
and then that workspace is
going to be cleared out.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:19.670
So you don't have any cruft
coming in from other builds.

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:22.370
The next thing you can
have is pre-emptable nodes.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:23.900
Pre-emptable nodes
allow you to save

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:29.800
70% of the cost of your nodes,
but there is a trade-off.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:34.060
At any point, those nodes could
be deprovisioned or terminated,

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:36.650
and they will only
last up to 24 hours.

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:38.699
So if you have some builds
that can be restarted,

00:12:38.699 --> 00:12:40.990
this can save you quite a
bit of money out of the gate.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.810
What we see is customers
running half of their nodes

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:47.600
as pre-emptable and another
half as regular nodes

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.451
so that they can always
have capacity for builds.

00:12:50.451 --> 00:12:52.450
The next thing that running
on Kubernetes engine

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:56.320
gives you is the ability to
apply soft and hard resource

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:56.840
limits.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:01.960
So if I say that a build needs
at least a gigabyte of RAM,

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:08.200
I can say that it also cannot
use more than two gigs of RAM.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:11.620
So I can set specifically how
much resources these builds

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:15.598
are going to use as
they share a node.

00:13:15.598 --> 00:13:17.510
Cool.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.140
So how do I define these
agents in my Jenkins file?

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:21.700
And this is something
that I advocate

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:25.210
for because you're giving that
control over to the developer.

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:26.740
You're saying, you
tell me how you

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:29.290
want your build spec to look.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:31.720
You have various containers
that you can run in here.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:35.410
And as you can see here, I'm
just defining a Kubernetes pod.

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:37.510
That means that I can
define multiple containers

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:41.060
if I need them and separate my
build dependencies in that way.

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:43.310
And that's really useful.

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:47.020
The other thing that I'm doing
down here is wrapping my shell

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:49.720
commands-- in this
example, go test--

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.940
with the container markup here.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:54.890
And that allows me to
say which container

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.530
each of these commands
should run in.

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:57.905
So really, you've
handed over all

00:13:57.905 --> 00:14:01.030
of the control of
the build process

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:05.700
to the developer in source
code, which is really awesome.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.480
So now that we've given them
control over their environment

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.570
and the build dependencies
that are in there,

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:14.180
we have to give them a way to
actually build those images.

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:15.860
The easiest way to
do that would be

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:18.230
to use Google Cloud
build, which allows

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:22.280
you to create a trigger
based off of source code.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.650
Then we'll build your
image and then upload that

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:26.420
to Google Container Registry.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:29.990
So a really streamlined process
for them to change their

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:31.258
build dependencies.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:38.800
Next, we said we want to
leave our artifacts off

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:40.090
of the Jenkins master.

00:14:40.090 --> 00:14:43.810
So we're going to install the
Google Cloud Storage plugin.

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:47.080
We're going to upload a
GCP IAM service account

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:49.420
key that has access
to the projects

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.132
that we want to upload
our artifacts to.

00:14:51.132 --> 00:14:52.840
And then, we're going
to use those in our

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:55.840
build process, usually
as opposed to build step,

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:59.590
to upload our artifacts
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:02.860
This can be done in the UI
or also as a pipeline step.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:05.800
Cool.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:07.900
So now, let's get into
the demo side of that,

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:13.300
and we're going to demo a
developer updating their image

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:16.640
and then pushing
a new build out.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:17.140
All right.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.980
So if we could go over
to the demo laptop,

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.387
yes, I am running
Linux on the desktop.

00:15:22.387 --> 00:15:23.970
Yes, it was extremely
difficult to get

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:26.520
this to show up on the screen.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.380
All right.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:30.760
So let's go to some code here.

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.360
So this is my developer
test image here.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.210
And really, it's just a
Docker file that has golang

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.190
and a couple of my
dependencies in there.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:38.730
Great.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:42.690
So let's say that I wanted to
use the golang rc build from

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.657
golang 1.11 instead of 1.10.

00:15:45.657 --> 00:15:47.240
I'm going to go ahead
and commit that.

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:54.560
All right.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:57.330
Golang 1.11.

00:15:57.330 --> 00:16:04.010
I'm going to tag it and push
that tag and the commit.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:10.830
So when I push
this, Cloud Build is

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.710
going to detect that I've
created a new commit,

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:16.120
and it's going to start
building my artifact.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:16.620
Great.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:18.453
So really, I didn't
have to do much in order

00:16:18.453 --> 00:16:19.370
to make that happen.

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:22.480
I could basically ignore the
fact that it did happen at all

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:25.530
and just start adding that
configuration of that base

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.630
image to my Jenkins file.

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:30.850
So in my Jenkins
file for my app,

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:35.340
I am defining the pod that
I want to run for my build.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.900
And in here, I have my test
image that I just showed you,

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:40.650
and I'm going to change
from version 6.0 to 7.0,

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.110
which is the one I
just built. And that's

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.980
really all I wanted to
change at this point.

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:46.479
So now, as a
developer, I can change

00:16:46.479 --> 00:16:49.680
my build setup relatively easy.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:53.490
And I'll just make that
change to my pipeline file

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:54.870
and commit and push that.

00:16:58.104 --> 00:17:00.420
Cool.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:01.710
All right.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.170
And once I push that, it's
going to go ahead and be

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:05.849
detected by Jenkins.

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:08.640
I'm actually just going to
kick a build off myself.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:10.410
Whoops.

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:12.250
Demo time.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:13.329
Let's see this here.

00:17:13.329 --> 00:17:16.650
Ahh, my port forwarding
wasn't working.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:17.200
Let's see.

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.655
So let's kick that off again.

00:17:19.655 --> 00:17:20.569
All right.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:25.040
And so now I've got my build
running inside of Jenkins,

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:28.349
and let's see what
this Jenkins file does.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:30.230
In here, I have
my pod definition.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:33.770
I'm defining two containers--
one for my test image,

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:36.800
and that's just really for
running gotest, and then

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:38.480
one for gcloud.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:41.030
And so, I don't have to
have this one mega uber

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.220
image for every single
thing that I want to have.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:45.050
I can compartmentalize
these dependencies

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:47.150
and then use them independently.

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.580
And Jenkins is going to
mount my workspace into each

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:51.080
of these containers,
so I don't have

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:53.210
to worry about
moving or mounting

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:55.170
weird workspaces anywhere.

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:59.310
So in my test
image, I run gotest.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.220
And then, after it's done
and the tests have passed--

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.670
which I'm sure were
really good tests--

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:10.380
I'm going to just run gcloud
container build submit in order

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:14.360
to build an image
from my docker file.

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:16.400
So let's take a look at
what's going on here.

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:20.470
So here, my build started.

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:22.600
We can see my
commit message here.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:25.540
Jenkins provisioned a pod
for me and then started

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:27.760
running my tests
and then the build.

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:33.640
So if I look in my
Kubernetes commands,

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:37.210
I can see that my
test pod was created.

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:38.010
So that's great.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:41.410
And now, it's being terminated
once the build has completed.

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:44.760
And that's because we had
our image built and pushed.

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:47.897
So our new image has been
deployed, and we're happy.

00:18:47.897 --> 00:18:49.030
Cool.

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:51.330
So that's the process of
a developer kind of owning

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:55.520
that CI process for themselves.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:00.310
So let's go back to the slides,
and we'll keep moving along.

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:04.990
So we talked about the CI
part of Jenkins on GKE.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:07.500
Now, we'll talking about
CD, Continuous Delivery

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:10.359
or Continuous Deployment.

00:19:10.359 --> 00:19:11.900
This is going to be
your best friend.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:14.470
This is kind of the
best tool we have today

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:17.270
and what we're going to
use throughout this demo

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:22.160
to talk about how to deploy
to Kubernetes from Jenkins.

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:25.154
How many people are using
kubectl apply to deploy?

00:19:25.154 --> 00:19:25.654
Yeah.

00:19:25.654 --> 00:19:27.977
OK.

00:19:27.977 --> 00:19:30.060
So the first thing you're
going to ask when you're

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:32.400
using a kubectl apply
or anything like this

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:34.802
is, which cluster should
I be deploying to?

00:19:34.802 --> 00:19:36.510
And in order to
understand that, we first

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:39.300
should understand
how kubeconfig works.

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:42.030
So the kubeconfig defines
a bunch of clusters,

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:46.180
and that's a name, a server
URL, and some CA data.

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:48.570
It also defines some users
and ways to authenticate

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:50.200
to those clusters.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:53.190
And it combines those two things
into what's called a context,

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:55.380
and the context is what
tells us definitively

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:58.230
who we should authenticate
with and which

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.331
cluster to authenticate to.

00:20:00.331 --> 00:20:01.830
Within your kubeconfig,
you're going

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:04.830
to have multiple of
these contexts lined up,

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.600
and one of them is
going to be flagged

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:07.627
as your current context.

00:20:07.627 --> 00:20:09.210
That's going to be
the default context

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:12.720
that when you run kubectl
with no other flags

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.751
is going to be used.

00:20:14.751 --> 00:20:15.250
Cool.

00:20:15.250 --> 00:20:16.708
So now that we know
that, how do we

00:20:16.708 --> 00:20:18.630
use kubeconfig
inside of Jenkins?

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:21.770
Well, they have a
handy plugin, again,

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:24.000
called the Kubernetes
CLI plugin.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:25.710
So in order to use
this, we're going

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:28.470
to upload our kubeconfig as
a Jenkins administrator--

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:30.480
as a Jenkins credential.

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:32.040
And then, within
our pipelines, we

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:36.480
can use that Jenkins credential
with this withKubeConfig

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.520
wrapping block
around our commands.

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:42.367
So that is what sets the context
for each of these commands

00:20:42.367 --> 00:20:43.450
that we run in that block.

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:45.210
So here, I have two
different stages,

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:48.030
one that deploys to canary,
one that applies to production.

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:49.530
And I can choose
which cluster it

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:53.170
is by setting the context name
in each of those withConfig

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:53.670
blocks.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:00.270
So now that we know
where to deploy code to,

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:02.514
how do we know how
to roll out the code?

00:21:02.514 --> 00:21:04.680
So for example, we could
do a blue-green deployment.

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.012
How many folks do
blue-green deployments?

00:21:07.012 --> 00:21:07.512
Some?

00:21:07.512 --> 00:21:08.380
OK.

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:11.710
So blue-green deployment
in Kubernetes,

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:13.540
without bringing
Jenkins into the mix,

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:15.927
you would have a service
and a deployment,

00:21:15.927 --> 00:21:18.010
and that service would map
to the deployment based

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:18.670
on the labels.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:22.460
So here, I have a label
app and a label version,

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:25.209
and those apply
to my deployment.

00:21:25.209 --> 00:21:27.250
So now, it's sending
traffic to the blue version.

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:28.833
In order to bring
on my green version,

00:21:28.833 --> 00:21:31.480
I would deploy it with a
different set of labels.

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:33.430
So here, I have
app my-app still,

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:35.360
but I've changed the
version to green.

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:39.340
So the service is no longer
mapping to that deployment.

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:41.950
But when I do want to map
it to that deployment,

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.770
I can switch the
label on the service

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.670
and do a blue-green
deployment that way.

00:21:45.670 --> 00:21:47.211
Now, if I want to
switch back, I just

00:21:47.211 --> 00:21:49.420
can switch the labels
on the service.

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:51.610
So that makes it easy to
do blue-green and then

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:52.940
roll back quickly.

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:53.440
All right.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:55.060
So we talked about
Jenkins there.

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:58.415
What would this look
like in a pipeline?

00:21:58.415 --> 00:22:00.290
Well, first, we would
want to build an image.

00:22:00.290 --> 00:22:02.890
You get an artifact out there.

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:05.320
Then, we want to create a
new deployment, which there

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:08.380
are many ways to do set and grep
and awk and all kinds of things

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:12.230
to get the right build
image into your deployment.

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:14.720
And once we do deploy
that new deployment,

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:17.080
we want to make sure
we verify it somehow.

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:18.130
And what does that mean?

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:21.790
Well, some things you could
do-- there's probably more--

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:24.710
is check that the rollout
has actually completed.

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:28.420
So you can use the kubectl
rollout status deployment

00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:30.255
command, and that
will tell you, yes, I

00:22:30.255 --> 00:22:31.630
think that I have
done everything

00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:33.460
that I need to do in
order to make sure

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:35.494
that this deployment rolls out.

00:22:35.494 --> 00:22:36.910
The next thing you
can do is check

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:39.400
that all your pods are
healthy and running

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:41.510
for some period of time after.

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.120
So you might run
into a situation

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:46.870
where you've
deployed a deployment

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:48.970
and the pods come up
healthy but then crash

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:50.300
after some amount of time.

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:53.680
So you might want to decide how
long that real healthy period

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:54.699
is going to be for you.

00:22:54.699 --> 00:22:56.740
And you can also define
some of this stuff inside

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:58.870
of the Kubernetes manifest.

00:22:58.870 --> 00:22:59.370
Cool.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:01.800
So now, we've verified
that our deployment has

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:03.570
succeeded as far as we know.

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:08.430
Now, we can shift traffic to
it using a similar mechanism.

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.590
And then, again,
we're going to verify

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:13.770
that, once we switch traffic
over, our service is happy.

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:15.490
So how are we going to do this?

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:17.610
We can use kubectl
endpoints to make sure

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:19.990
that pods are actually
mapped to our service.

00:23:19.990 --> 00:23:21.930
And this would be
something to verify

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:24.090
that your labeling
scheme is correct.

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:26.310
You may have put some bad
labels in that deployment,

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:29.297
and now your service doesn't
know where to send traffic.

00:23:29.297 --> 00:23:31.130
The next thing you can
do is go one level up

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:32.254
on the load balancing side.

00:23:32.254 --> 00:23:34.800
And if you've had any HTTP
load balancers provisioned

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:37.140
with an ingress
resource, you can

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:40.290
use kubectl get ingress to make
sure your backends are still

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.020
healthy.

00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:43.520
Once those are
complete and you know

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.430
your service is mapped to a
load balancer appropriately,

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:48.590
you can start to run tests.

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:50.960
Maybe it's kubectl run.

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:52.580
Maybe you're hitting
it from outside.

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:54.380
But actually verify
that your service

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:56.730
is doing what it's supposed
to be doing in some way,

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:57.980
shape, or form.

00:23:57.980 --> 00:23:59.780
If these things fail,
you can roll back

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:03.490
to the previous
deployment at this point.

00:24:03.490 --> 00:24:06.610
If everything succeeds, then you
delete your previous deployment

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:07.940
and move along your merry way.

00:24:10.769 --> 00:24:12.810
So that's one way to
deploy, which is blue-green.

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:15.524
How many people
do rolling update?

00:24:15.524 --> 00:24:16.190
A good majority.

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:19.360
Your application has to be
understanding of a rolling

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:21.820
update, but it's a great way
to deploy and the default way

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:23.740
to deploy within Kubernetes.

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.020
We will bring up
new pods for you

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:28.720
and tear down your
old ones one by one.

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:31.150
You do have some flexibility
in how you roll out

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:34.150
when you do that strategy,
and your options here

00:24:34.150 --> 00:24:37.960
are how many pods should I
should you bring up at a time,

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:42.530
and then how many pods can be
unavailable at any given time.

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:46.070
These two can be used
with either percentages

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:47.190
or absolute values.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:49.310
And I think, for
larger deployments,

00:24:49.310 --> 00:24:50.960
you're going to want
to do percentages.

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:52.340
For things that are
relatively static,

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:53.756
you might be able
to just get away

00:24:53.756 --> 00:24:56.724
with using a number of pods.

00:24:56.724 --> 00:24:57.600
Cool.

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:02.300
So what can I do further to
make this deployment robust?

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:05.390
One thing is the readiness
and liveness probes

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:06.680
that you attach to your pods.

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:09.900
How many people use
readiness probes?

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:12.090
This is probably the
most important thing

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:15.540
you can do to have a
robust deployment process.

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:16.920
What is a readiness probe?

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.980
The readiness probe is
a way for Kubernetes

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.411
to ask your app if it
should send traffic to it.

00:25:23.411 --> 00:25:24.660
And that's a really key place.

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:28.092
It's where your application
and the infrastructure meet,

00:25:28.092 --> 00:25:29.550
and you have to
have something that

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:33.680
is very indicative of your
service's health responding

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:34.180
here.

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:38.010
So for example, you can
have an HTTP request.

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:40.470
Some people have
a health endpoint.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:42.330
You can have all kinds
of different things

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:44.800
that are checked
in this endpoint.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:46.680
And if, for example,
your application

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:49.380
needs to reach
out to a database,

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:51.180
make sure that that
database is there

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.610
before you respond that you're
ready to receive traffic.

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.920
So you can think of all of
the different dependencies

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:58.380
in your application that
would need to be there

00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:02.910
for you to have a really healthy
request or a healthy response

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:04.350
to a request that came in.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:06.780
This is really something really
important to think about,

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:08.238
and there's a lot
of tuning you can

00:26:08.238 --> 00:26:12.880
do to get the right
readiness probe in place.

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:16.260
The next thing is
graceful termination.

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:19.470
So when your pod is going to
get taken down by Kubernetes,

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:22.410
it's going to send your
pod and your containers

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:26.170
a SIGTERM signal, and you
have to react to that signal

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:28.350
because it's basically
Kubernetes-- again,

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:30.630
the application and
infrastructure-- coming

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:33.270
together to say, I am
going to tear you down.

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:35.010
You need to stop doing
what you're doing

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:37.051
and make sure that all
your requests get flushed,

00:26:37.051 --> 00:26:38.920
for example.

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:42.960
So one example of a good way
to handle the SIGTERM, when

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:45.690
you receive that SIGTERM,
make sure that you turn off

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:48.650
or disable your readiness
probe so that Kubernetes

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.595
stops sending you traffic.

00:26:50.595 --> 00:26:52.470
If you're going to be
torn down, you probably

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:55.840
shouldn't have any more
traffic coming at you.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:58.420
After that point, you
can start to finish up

00:26:58.420 --> 00:26:59.980
any connections
you have and then

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:01.522
terminate yourself gracefully.

00:27:01.522 --> 00:27:02.980
One of the options
you have here is

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:05.200
the
terminationGracePeriodSeconds,

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.870
and that's going to allow you
to define how long of time

00:27:07.870 --> 00:27:11.530
you need in order to tear down.

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:12.610
Great.

00:27:12.610 --> 00:27:14.650
So we've talked about a
lot of different things

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:16.300
that you can do
in your CD process

00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:20.657
using Jenkins and kubectl
apply in your pipeline files.

00:27:20.657 --> 00:27:22.490
And now, we'll look at
a different approach,

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:24.770
which is using Spinnaker.

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:28.800
Spinnaker is a multi-cloud
continuous delivery platform.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:30.780
Jenkins started
out as a CI server.

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:33.780
It can run anything
that you need it to run.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.880
Spinnaker started
out as fully focused

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:37.740
on continuous
delivery-- only how

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:41.640
to get code and artifact
from one place to another.

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:48.150
So if continuous delivery is
this long chain of events,

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:50.900
Jenkins is extremely
good at this part

00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:53.400
and Spinnaker is extremely
good at the next part, which

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:55.680
is actually getting
your code rolled out

00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:59.580
into various environments,
doing manual judgments based

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:03.790
on whether certain metrics
are looking correct.

00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:07.470
And so this is what
we're going to see today.

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:10.560
There are some interfaces
between Jenkins and Spinnaker,

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:14.400
and the two main ones are
that Spinnaker pipelines can

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:17.580
be triggered by a Jenkins job.

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:20.400
So as a Jenkins job
completes successfully,

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.380
Spinnaker will check
it and potentially

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:24.720
take some information
from that build result

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:28.364
and then start its pipeline
with that information.

00:28:28.364 --> 00:28:30.780
The other thing that you can
do with Spinnaker and Jenkins

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:34.920
is have Spinnaker
run a Jenkins job

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:36.640
in the middle of a pipeline.

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:38.730
So you may have
a rollout process

00:28:38.730 --> 00:28:41.490
that requires some sort of
performance test in the middle,

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:44.220
and that really is
orchestrated or run by Jenkins.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:47.880
So you can have Spinnaker do
a rollout of a deployment,

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:50.610
run your integration test
or your performance test

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:53.010
using Jenkins, and then
continue your rollout.

00:28:53.010 --> 00:28:57.750
So you don't always have to
pick all of them up all at once.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:29:00.150
So in Spinnaker,
you have the ability

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:02.580
to define these triggers,
and the pipeline

00:29:02.580 --> 00:29:04.290
trigger UI looks like this.

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:10.350
You define which Jenkins
master you want to point at,

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:17.510
which job you want to detect
a change in from that Jenkins

00:29:17.510 --> 00:29:21.890
master, and then potentially
a properties file.

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:24.800
And so the properties file is a
way to communicate information

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:26.990
from Jenkins into
Spinnaker, and that

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:31.310
follows the Java properties
file format, which is key

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:32.940
equals value.

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:35.810
And so you can pass-- if you
create this file inside of your

00:29:35.810 --> 00:29:38.240
build process and
archive it, then

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:40.970
Spinnaker can pick it up
and use this information

00:29:40.970 --> 00:29:43.250
in its pipeline to
configure various steps.

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:49.390
The other thing that I said
that Spinnaker could do

00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:52.354
is kick off a Jenkins job
in the middle of a pipeline.

00:29:52.354 --> 00:29:54.020
So the way that this
looks is, let's say

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:58.600
I have a Jenkins job that
has these parameters.

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:00.970
In Spinnaker, as
part of my pipeline,

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:04.860
I can create a stage that
will pull in this Jenkins job

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:06.280
and show me those parameters.

00:30:06.280 --> 00:30:08.250
It will bring up the
defaults as well.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:11.520
But then, I can parametrize
them with information

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:13.420
from my Spinnaker pipeline.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:15.620
So these two
interplay pretty well,

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:17.920
and so you don't have to
give up Jenkins in order

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:21.840
to use Spinnaker or vice versa.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:24.200
So now, we'll go through
what this process actually

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:27.830
looks like end-to-end, with
Jenkins doing most of the CI

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:30.530
work and then Spinnaker
picking up from there

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:33.680
once an artifact is built.
So if we could switch over

00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:40.410
to the laptop, once I log in.

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:46.920
OK.

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:51.564
So here, I mentioned, I
had a web app, sample app.

00:30:51.564 --> 00:30:53.730
Here, we're going to do a
green-blue deployment just

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:55.680
to be a little different.

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:56.970
Our UI is green.

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:59.460
Some product manager comes
in with their hair on fire

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:00.930
and says green is no longer in.

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:02.820
We need this to be blue ASAP.

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:04.660
Developer, make it happen.

00:31:04.660 --> 00:31:08.850
So I use my awesome coding
skills to hack HTML.

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:17.680
And I go to my green, change
that to blue wherever I see it.

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:18.537
And I am pumped.

00:31:18.537 --> 00:31:19.870
I don't know what this error is.

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:22.730
We're going to ignore it.

00:31:22.730 --> 00:31:24.870
That may be my downfall
on this whole demo thing,

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:26.321
but we'll see.

00:31:26.321 --> 00:31:26.820
All right.

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:33.669
So I commit this, make it
blue, and I'm going to push it.

00:31:33.669 --> 00:31:35.960
And then, we're going to
follow this along a little bit

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:37.680
as it goes through our process.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:40.380
So again, I'm just
going to kick this off.

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:42.140
So we get a build ASAP.

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:45.770
Awesome.

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:47.510
So first thing that's
going to happen

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:49.550
is Jenkins says,
hey, I don't have

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:51.140
any pods available for you.

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:52.340
I need to provision one.

00:31:52.340 --> 00:31:54.920
So we should see in here
that Jenkins is going

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:56.830
to start provisioning me a pod.

00:31:56.830 --> 00:31:57.470
Cool.

00:31:57.470 --> 00:32:00.149
So it provisions me a
pod just for that build.

00:32:00.149 --> 00:32:02.690
And it's going to run through
our procedure, which is, again,

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:04.924
to run our tests--

00:32:04.924 --> 00:32:07.340
well, first check out our
source code, then run our tests,

00:32:07.340 --> 00:32:09.500
and then do a build
with Cloud Build.

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:11.990
And then, in Cloud
Build, I'll just show you

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:14.086
the trigger that I set up.

00:32:14.086 --> 00:32:15.210
Oh, I don't have a trigger.

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:18.480
I'm just building them directly.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:22.020
In Spinnaker, I
have a trigger here

00:32:22.020 --> 00:32:25.560
that is waiting
for changes to GCR.

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:29.640
So any image that comes in into
a particular GCR repository,

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:31.244
it's going to kick
off this pipeline.

00:32:31.244 --> 00:32:32.660
And then, I tell
it that I'm going

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:36.690
to use that docker
image that got pushed

00:32:36.690 --> 00:32:39.000
as part of a pipeline step.

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:42.430
So in that pipeline, I deploy my
backend and deploy my frontend.

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:47.760
Right now, I have a static
artifact, which I just

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:51.590
enter text, but I could pull
this from a repository or a GCS

00:32:51.590 --> 00:32:52.500
bucket.

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:55.100
And I tell it that
I want to take

00:32:55.100 --> 00:32:57.990
these artifacts,
or this manifest,

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:02.590
and replace this image that's
in here with the one that

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:06.310
came in from the docker build.

00:33:06.310 --> 00:33:10.840
So here, I have
my builds running.

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:13.760
And you'll see in
here that Spinnaker

00:33:13.760 --> 00:33:16.280
is doing a lot of those
things that we scripted out

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:18.190
in our pipeline for us.

00:33:18.190 --> 00:33:19.670
It's monitoring the deployment.

00:33:19.670 --> 00:33:21.950
It's making sure that things
actually get to the state

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:23.120
that they want to get to.

00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:26.990
Spinnaker actually understands
the infrastructure behind it.

00:33:26.990 --> 00:33:29.990
So it knows what a pod is
and what it means for the pod

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:31.500
to be healthy and so forth.

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:33.500
So that's what's running
in the background here.

00:33:37.341 --> 00:33:37.840
There we go.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:38.798
So now, it's succeeded.

00:33:38.798 --> 00:33:41.640
It's actually pushed
out our change,

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:43.057
and we should have blue.

00:33:43.057 --> 00:33:44.890
And that error didn't
mean anything, so yay.

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:50.790
So if we could go
back to slides,

00:33:50.790 --> 00:33:53.490
that's a very simplified view
of something you could do.

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:56.220
Spinnaker can do much
more complex deployments

00:33:56.220 --> 00:33:59.980
to multiple clusters, do manual
judgments, all kinds of stuff,

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:02.250
but I just wanted you to
see the connective tissue

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:03.312
between these pieces.

00:34:03.312 --> 00:34:05.520
And you can extrapolate
forward with your imagination

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:07.144
as to what your build
process should be

00:34:07.144 --> 00:34:09.560
and your deployment
process should be.

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:10.620
OK, Vic.

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:12.710
So you just gave me
all this information.

00:34:12.710 --> 00:34:14.070
You're probably going to
launch this on YouTube

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:16.060
again to probably dig
into some of the pieces,

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:18.239
but what can you
do today to start

00:34:18.239 --> 00:34:20.820
getting your hands dirty
with some of these processes?

00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:23.949
I mentioned earlier that I've
written a lot about Jenkins

00:34:23.949 --> 00:34:26.159
and GKE, and so I'll show
you a few of the things

00:34:26.159 --> 00:34:26.909
that I've written.

00:34:26.909 --> 00:34:30.989
First was the Jenkins on GKE
Continuous Delivery tutorial,

00:34:30.989 --> 00:34:34.560
and this is really
using multiple branches

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.870
to define environments
and then having

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:40.469
Jenkins push those environments
out into Container Engine.

00:34:40.469 --> 00:34:43.280
And that is available
online there.

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:46.310
That's Jenkins on GKE
Continuous Delivery.

00:34:46.310 --> 00:34:49.230
I also wrote one with just
Container Builder, or Cloud

00:34:49.230 --> 00:34:51.929
Build, as it's been
renamed, and that

00:34:51.929 --> 00:34:56.340
is Continuous Delivery with
Spinnaker on Kubernetes Engine.

00:34:56.340 --> 00:34:58.950
So that's if you want to use
Spinnaker and Cloud Build

00:34:58.950 --> 00:35:01.810
for your CI and CD process.

00:35:01.810 --> 00:35:03.700
But then, there are
some folks who are not

00:35:03.700 --> 00:35:06.640
ready to adopt Kubernetes yet.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:09.970
And for those folks, I have
the Jenkins on GCE tutorial.

00:35:09.970 --> 00:35:12.580
And this is, I think,
the easiest way

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:14.470
to get going with
those build agents

00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:17.290
that I said before that
automatically autoscale and get

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:19.990
provisioned when you need them.

00:35:19.990 --> 00:35:23.630
You can just basically take your
even on-prem Jenkins master,

00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:26.320
enable the Jenkins GCE
plugin, and get going

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:28.340
with distributed builds on GCE.

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:31.960
So a really easy way to onboard.

00:35:31.960 --> 00:35:32.920
And that is it.

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:35.040
Thank you so much
for listening to me.

00:35:35.040 --> 00:35:35.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:40.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

